t[1],\\n        'summed_score': best[0]\\n    }\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to parse the problem. It's a budgeted sentence selection problem. The case has lengths, scores, and budget. We need to select a subset of sentences (indices) such that sum of lengths <= budget, maximize sum of scores. If multiple subsets have same total score, prefer fewer total tokens used, then lexicographic source indices (presumably ascending order? The original description says \"lexicographic source indices\" but we need to interpret. The sentences are in order; lexicographic comparison of sequences of indices? Typically, if two selections have same total score and same total tokens, then compare the list of selected indices in ascending order (since document order) and choose the one that is lexicographically smaller (i.e., earlier indices preferred). But need to confirm.\n\nAlso need to preserve document order: the selected indices must be in increasing order (since it's reconstructing context). The DP will naturally preserve order if we consider items in order.\n\nWe need to return: selected indices, used_tokens (total length of selected), summed_score.\n\nWe also need to check budget feasibility: if the smallest single sentence length exceeds budget, then infeasible? Actually if no subset can be selected because all sentences exceed budget, then it's infeasible. But budget might be zero? Probably budget >= min length? We should check: if sum of lengths of all sentences is less than budget, that's fine; we can select all. But if every sentence length > budget, then we can't select any. Should we consider selecting empty set? The problem says \"select and reconstruct context-scored sentences under an exact token budget\". Usually you must select at least something? But if budget is less than any sentence length, then no sentence can be selected, and perhaps that's infeasible. However, typical DP allows empty selection with total score 0. But the specification says \"Budgeted sen