rce indices.\" So if two states have same score, prefer one with fewer tokens. That is consistent with minimizing token usage. But note that states at different token counts can have same score; we must keep the one with fewer tokens. So in DP, we store for each possible token consumption the best. But also we might have states that use different token counts but same score? At the end, we need the best overall: maximum score, then minimum tokens, then lexicographic indices. So we can collect all states (0..budget) and pick the best.\n\nBut careful: The DP table might have multiple states with same token count. We keep only the best for each token count. For states with different token counts, we compare using the tie-breaking rule.\n\nGiven that the problem's objective is to maximize summed score, not necessarily to use exactly budget. So we need to consider all possible token usages up to budget, and pick the best.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Validate inputs: lengths and scores must be lists of same length, budget non-negative integer.\n- Possibly check that all lengths are positive integers? They are given as ints. scores are floats. Budget is int.\n- If budget < 0: raise ValueError.\n- If lengths contain non-positive? Probably not, but we can check.\n- If any length > budget and there is no other smaller length? But we handle automatically.\n\nDP:\nn = len(lengths)\nstates = {0: (0, [])}  # token_used -> (score, indices_list)\nFor i in range(n):\n    length = lengths[i]\n    score = scores[i]\n    new_states = {}\n    for used, (sc, idxs) in states.items():\n        # keep original\n        if used not in new_states or better(sc, len(idxs), idxs, new_states[used][0], len(new_states[used][1]), new_states[used][1]):\n            new_states[used] = (sc, idxs[:])  # copy? Actually we will update in place? Better to copy.\n        # add sentence\n        new_used = used + length\n        if new_used <= budget:\n            new_sc = sc + score\n            new_idxs 